// EXPECT_STDOUT sum=3
// Registry 221.1 #799 (the NAME-COLLISION half; the interpolation half is
// `m799_default_arg_call_in_spawn.nv`, 2026-08-29).
//
// A call carrying a DEFAULTED parameter, made from inside a `spawn` body, where
// the CALLER'S variable bears THE SAME NAME as one of the CALLEE'S parameters.
//
// Mechanism, read in the generated C rather than guessed. `callnorm` (D102 F.2)
// rewrites the call into a Block whose locals are NAMED AFTER THE CALLEE'S
// PARAMETERS -- deliberately, so default expressions resolve without a
// substitution walk. Phase 1 of that Block emits `__nova_arg_src0 = policy`,
// reading the CALLER's `policy`; phase 2 then declares a local `policy`. Inside
// a spawn the "capture or local" decision is made FLATLY (`refs - bound`), so
// the name lands in `bound`, no field for it appears in the fiber context
// struct at all, and the phase-1 read resolves to a local that is not declared
// until the next line:
//
//     error: use of undeclared identifier 'policy'
//         NovaValue_Pol __nova_arg_src0 = policy;
//
// WHY THE FIX RENAMES INSTEAD OF FIXING THE CAPTURE SET. Collecting the capture
// is not enough: the reference rewrite is flat TOO, so `policy` would become
// `_c->policy` EVERYWHERE in the body -- including the use that sits AFTER the
// local's declaration and must read the LOCAL. That trades a loud CC-FAIL for a
// silently wrong value. Renaming removes the collision both mechanisms trip
// over, and touches neither.
//
// THE COLLISION IS THE WHOLE TRIGGER, and it is measured, not assumed: the same
// shape with the names spread apart (`pol` / `p`) builds and runs on the
// pre-fix binary. Both carriers are kept side by side in
// `docs/plans/repro/p799/` -- they differ by ONE variable name.
//
// The statement-level form is deliberate: this is the half the interpolation
// fixture does NOT cover, and vice versa.
module standalone.m799_name_collision_in_spawn

type Pol value { a int, b int, c int }

fn callee(policy Pol, stop Option[int] = None) -> int {
    match stop { Some(v) => policy.a + v, None => policy.a + policy.b + policy.c }
}

fn main() Io -> () {
    ro policy = Pol { a: 1, b: 1, c: 1 }
    ro (tx, rx) = Channel[int].new(1)
    supervised {
        spawn {
            ro n = callee(policy)
            ro _ = tx.try_send(n)
        }
    }
    match rx.recv() {
        Some(v) => print("sum=${v}")
        None    => print("no value")
    }
}
